# Stable names for Doggobot's USB serial devices.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the Pi has the VESC, the LD06 LiDAR and now the pan-axis
# ESP32 all enumerating as /dev/ttyUSB*. Kernel numbering follows the order the
# devices happen to be probed at boot, which is not a promise and does change
# when something is replugged or powers up slowly. all.launch.py currently hands
# /dev/ttyUSB0 to the LiDAR by name; if the ESP32 ever wins that number, the
# LiDAR driver opens the servo bus and the safety node goes blind. Naming each
# device after its own hardware serial removes the race entirely.
#
# INSTALL
#   sudo cp deploy/99-doggobot-serial.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger
#   ls -l /dev/doggobot-*
#
# TWO pan boards exist and either may be fitted, so both are matched and both
# claim the same name. Only one is ever plugged in at a time.
#
# 1. The board on the car (2026-09-01): an ESP32-S3 with NATIVE USB, so it
#    presents as Espressif's own USB-Serial/JTAG unit rather than through a
#    separate UART chip, and enumerates as ttyACM rather than ttyUSB. Its
#    "serial" is the chip's MAC address, which is unique per part.
#    Note it also needs CDCOnBoot=cdc in the firmware build: with native USB,
#    a stock build leaves Serial on the hardware UART and the port stays silent.
# ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE keeps ModemManager off it. It probes new ttyACM devices
# looking for a modem, which means writing AT commands at a servo controller and
# holding the port while it does so.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="303a", ATTRS{idProduct}=="1001", ATTRS{serial}=="10:20:BA:0D:FA:C8", SYMLINK+="doggobot-pan", MODE="0666", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1"

# 2. The bench board (2026-08-30): an ESP32-S3 behind an FTDI FT232R, so it
#    enumerates as ttyUSB. FT232R serials are unique per chip.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6001", ATTRS{serial}=="A5069RR4", SYMLINK+="doggobot-pan", MODE="0666"

# Fill these in the same way once their serials are read on the Pi:
#   udevadm info -n /dev/ttyUSB0 | grep -E 'ID_VENDOR_ID|ID_MODEL_ID|ID_SERIAL_SHORT'
# Read off the car 2026-09-01. The LiDAR's CP2102 reports serial "0001", which
# is a default rather than a unique id, so it is matched on vendor and product
# alone: fine while it is the only CP2102 on the machine.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", SYMLINK+="doggobot-lidar", MODE="0666"
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0483", ATTRS{idProduct}=="5740", ATTRS{serial}=="304", SYMLINK+="doggobot-vesc", MODE="0666"
